+<!-- ============ OVERVIEW ============ -->
+<section class="view" id="v-overview">
+<div class="wrap">
+  <div class="eyebrow">Design document · v0.1 draft</div>
+  <h1>Integration360</h1>
+  <p class="lede">A read-only observability layer over every integration between BBH and the SEI Wealth Platform. It answers one question, for one business date, across four channels: <strong>did everything integrate correctly?</strong></p>
+
+  <div class="note">
+    <span class="note-h">The scope in one line</span>
+    <p>Integration360 observes. It never moves data, never remediates, never writes to a pipeline table. The CP Integration Hub owns quarantine, error handling and retry; Integration360 shows what the Hub is holding and why.</p>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2>The three questions</h2>
+  <p>Every screen answers one of three questions, for a given channel and business date. This is the organising spine of the whole design — if a view doesn't answer one of them, it doesn't belong.</p>
+
+  <div class="grid2">
+    <div class="card">
+      <h4>Completeness</h4>
+      <p>Did everything that should have arrived, arrive — and everything that should have gone, go? Measured against an expectation, not against yesterday.</p>
+    </div>
+    <div class="card">
+      <h4>Timeliness</h4>
+      <p>Did it happen inside the window? Requires knowing what was <em>due</em>, which today no channel records. See <a href="#open" data-jump="open">Assumptions</a>.</p>
+    </div>
+    <div class="card">
+      <h4>Correctness</h4>
+      <p>Do the counts reconcile at every boundary, and what is held in exception? Reconciliation is derived here, not read.</p>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2>What it does and does not do</h2>
+  <div class="tscroll"><table>
+    <thead><tr><th style="width:50%">In scope</th><th>Out of scope — and who owns it</th></tr></thead>
+    <tbody>
+      <tr><td>Show state of every channel per business date</td><td>Moving or transforming data — <strong>Airflow + dbt</strong></td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Show exceptions: what failed, why, with what code</td><td>Resolving, waiving, retrying an exception — <strong>Integration Hub</strong></td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Derive SLA and reconciliation verdicts, with history</td><td>Quarantine and correction handling — <strong>Integration Hub</strong></td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Observe the outbound loop: SEI's pushed status, and the Hub's backstop poll of SEI</td><td>Submitting to SEI, generating payloads — <strong>Integration Hub</strong></td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Reconcile the standby file against the event stream</td><td>Alerting on pipeline-owned failures — <strong>Splunk</strong></td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Correlate across Oracle, Airflow, the Hub and the inbox</td><td>Anything inside SEI beyond what SEI publishes</td></tr>
+    </tbody>
+  </table></div>
+
+  <h2>Why it owns almost nothing</h2>
+  <p>Integration360's own schema is five tables, and none of them hold integration state. Everything it shows is read from the system that owns it. That is what makes the observability-only claim structural rather than aspirational — there is no table in this design that another system would need to read back.</p>
+
+  <div class="legend">
+    <span><span class="comp-dot d-ok"></span> Specified — enough information to build</span>
+    <span><span class="comp-dot d-warn"></span> Assumed — built on a stated assumption</span>
+    <span><span class="comp-dot d-stop"></span> Blocked — needs an answer first</span>
+    <span><span class="comp-dot d-ext"></span> Not owned — read or shared</span>
+  </div>
+  <p style="font-size:12.5px;color:var(--ink-3);margin-top:9px">Every component carries one of these states. Four are blocked — the Hub’s Foundation framework resolved three of the seven, by giving Integration360 a threshold store, an expectation store and an estate-wide error model to read.</p>
+</div>
+</section>
