+  <h2>Actors and systems</h2>
+  <div class="tscroll"><table>
+    <thead><tr><th style="width:23%">Element</th><th style="width:13%">Owner</th><th>Relationship to Integration360</th></tr></thead>
+    <tbody>
+      <tr><td><strong>BBH Operations</strong></td><td>BBH</td><td>The only audience. Reads; cannot act from within the tool. <span class="pill p-stop">no user research yet</span></td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Oracle control schema</strong></td><td>Airflow + dbt</td><td>Read. File registry, date control, micro-batch registry, DQ store, reconciliation results.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Airflow metadata</strong></td><td>Platform</td><td>Read, <strong>replica only</strong>. Task-level state for failure attribution. Never the scheduler's primary.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>CP Integration Hub</strong></td><td>BBH</td><td>Read. Outbound submissions, quarantine, transport errors, remediation state.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Notification inbox</strong></td><td>Shared platform</td><td>Read. Not Integration360's table — the Hub consumes it to act, Integration360 to display.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>dbt run results</strong></td><td>dbt</td><td>Read. Model-level outcomes and test results, persisted to Oracle by an <code>on-run-end</code> hook.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>SEI PS-Orchestration</strong></td><td>SEI</td><td>Pushes loader status and errors through the BBH Apigee proxy into the inbox.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>SEI Data Cloud</strong></td><td>SEI</td><td>No relationship. Events reach the Event Listener; Integration360 reads the listener's staging, never the Hub itself.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Splunk</strong></td><td>Pipeline</td><td>Excluded. Write-only from the pipeline; Integration360 never queries it.</td></tr>
+    </tbody>
+  </table></div>
+
+  <div class="note warn">
+    <span class="note-h">The consequence of excluding Splunk</span>
+    <p>The pipeline publishes its own SLA and reconciliation verdicts to Splunk. Integration360 derives its own from Oracle. Two independent computations of the same judgement will drift unless both read the same threshold — which is why every verdict this system stores also stores <strong>the threshold that was in force when it was computed</strong>.</p>
+  </div>
+</div>
+</section>
