+  <h2>Container responsibilities</h2>
+  <div class="tscroll"><table>
+    <thead><tr><th style="width:19%">Container</th><th style="width:15%">Technology</th><th>Responsibility</th><th style="width:11%">State</th></tr></thead>
+    <tbody>
+      <tr><td><strong>Web UI</strong></td><td>React SPA, mounted as a CP360 nav entry</td><td>Renders the estate board, four channel views, the exception list, reconciliation and the business-date timeline. Deep-links out to Airflow and the CP360 catalogue rather than reproducing them.</td><td><span class="pill p-warn">assumed</span></td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Read API</strong></td><td>FastAPI; routers mounted by name in <code>main.py</code></td><td>Synchronous reads. Owns the source adapters, the correlation layer that assembles across databases, status normalisation and redaction on read.</td><td><span class="pill p-ok">specified</span></td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Observation Engine</strong></td><td>Python, scheduled</td><td>Computes verdicts with the threshold in force, builds rollups, runs the cross-channel reconciliation, detects gaps nothing else sees. The only writer to the store.</td><td><span class="pill p-ok">specified</span></td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>INT360 Store</strong></td><td>Oracle schema <code>INT360_*</code></td><td>Five tables: verdict snapshots, daily rollups, alert log, saved views, optional annotations. No integration state.</td><td><span class="pill p-ok">specified</span></td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Alert Dispatcher</strong></td><td>TBD — email, Teams or ServiceNow</td><td>The only path from a finding to a human. Needed because the Hub alerts on what it manages and Splunk on what the pipeline emits; neither covers what Integration360 alone detects.</td><td><span class="pill p-stop">blocked</span></td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Notification Inbox</strong></td><td>Oracle table, shared platform</td><td>Not owned. SEI writes through the Apigee proxy; the Hub consumes it to remediate, Integration360 to display. Each consumer tracks its own progress marker.</td><td><span class="pill p-ext">shared</span></td></tr>
+    </tbody>
+  </table></div>
+
+  <div class="note">
+    <span class="note-h">Why the inbox is shared rather than owned</span>
+    <p>If SEI pushed to Integration360 and the Hub had to obtain the content from it, an observability tool would sit on the critical path for remediation. Placing the inbox behind the Apigee proxy and in front of both consumers keeps one endpoint for SEI while letting Integration360 restart, redeploy and fail over without anyone noticing.</p>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2>Deployment notes</h2>
+  <div class="grid2">
+    <div class="card"><h4>Network zone</h4><p>Integration360 runs in BBH infrastructure. It is reachable by BBH operations only; the SEI push terminates at the Apigee proxy and the inbox, never at this application.</p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Database access</h4><p>A dedicated read-only account per source. Airflow is read from a <strong>replica</strong> — an analytical query against the scheduler's primary will degrade scheduling.</p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Landing zone</h4><p>The cross-channel check parses a standby file's trailer. That requires a read mount, or an upstream component publishing the count. A deployment decision, not a detail.</p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Airflow schema coupling</h4><p>The metadata schema is not a public API and changes between versions. All Airflow queries sit behind one adapter so an upgrade is a single-file change.</p></div>
+  </div>
+</div>
+</section>
