+
+  <div class="planes" id="planes">
+
+  <!-- PLANE: UI -->
+  <div class="plane">
+    <div class="plane-hd">
+      <div class="plane-t"><span class="glyph"></span>Presentation · Web UI</div>
+      <div class="plane-meta">8 components · 2 blocked</div>
+    </div>
+    <div class="comps">
+      <button class="comp" aria-expanded="false"><span class="comp-dot d-warn"></span><span class="comp-n">EstateBoard</span></button>
+      <div class="detail" hidden>
+        <h5>EstateBoard</h5>
+        <div class="dmeta"><span class="pill p-warn">assumed</span><span>the landing view</span></div>
+        <p>Four channels by three questions, for one business date. Twelve cells, each green, amber or red, each clicking through to the channel view beneath it. The whole product in one screen — if an operator cannot tell from this grid whether the night went well, nothing else matters.</p>
+        <dl>
+          <dt>Reads</dt><dd><code>GET /int360/estate?date=</code></dd>
+          <dt>Source</dt><dd>Rollups and verdict snapshots only — never live source tables</dd>
+          <dt>Assumption</dt><dd>That the morning question is "did last night work". Unverified — no user research exists.</dd>
+        </dl>
+      </div>
+
+      <button class="comp" aria-expanded="false"><span class="comp-dot d-warn"></span><span class="comp-n">EventChannelView</span></button>
+      <div class="detail" hidden>
+        <h5>EventChannelView</h5>
+        <div class="dmeta"><span class="pill p-warn">assumed</span><span>primary ingestion path</span></div>
+        <p>Micro-batches for the date, with consumer lag per partition, sequence gaps, marker arrival against expectation, and the pull-complete state that actually gates the business date.</p>
+        <dl>
+          <dt>Reads</dt><dd><code>GET /int360/event/{date}</code></dd>
+          <dt>Shows</dt><dd>micro-batch timeline · lag sparkline per partition · sequence gap list · marker grid by domain</dd>
+          <dt>Limit</dt><dd>Because the event carries no data and the pull re-reads current state, this view shows <strong>that</strong> a key changed, never what changed. Flow health, not lineage.</dd>
+        </dl>
+      </div>
+
+      <button class="comp" aria-expanded="false"><span class="comp-dot d-ok"></span><span class="comp-n">StandbyFileView</span></button>
+      <div class="detail" hidden>
+        <h5>StandbyFileView</h5>
+        <div class="dmeta"><span class="pill p-ok">specified</span><span>fallback readiness</span></div>
+        <p>Files arrive daily and are deliberately not loaded. This view answers a different question from the event view: not <em>did the data arrive</em>, but <em>is the parachute packed</em>. Three states per interface — absent, arrived-and-held, unavailable — plus the trailer count used for the cross-channel check.</p>
+        <dl>
+          <dt>Reads</dt><dd><code>GET /int360/file/{date}</code></dd>
+          <dt>Source</dt><dd><code>FILE_REGISTRY</code> plus the cross-channel reconciliation row</dd>
+          <dt>Watch</dt><dd>The seven-state lifecycle has no terminal value meaning "available and deliberately unused". Until one exists, standby files look either missing or falsely complete.</dd>
+        </dl>
+      </div>
+
+      <button class="comp" aria-expanded="false"><span class="comp-dot d-ok"></span><span class="comp-n">OutboundView</span></button>
+      <div class="detail" hidden>
+        <h5>OutboundView</h5>
+        <div class="dmeta"><span class="pill p-ok">specified</span><span>two status sources on one row</span></div>
+        <p>The submission lifecycle per loader type — generated, submitted, notified, terminal — carrying submitted, accepted and rejected counts. Never a boolean, because partial acceptance is the norm and Unified Cash and Free Movement carry record-level approval. Every status transition shows whether it arrived by push or by poll.</p>
+        <dl>
+          <dt>Unblocked by</dt><dd>The mechanism is settled: SEI pushes a notification, BBH polls SEI for reject detail and as a backstop. A terminal state found only by the poller — with no notification — is displayed as such, and is the running measure of the push channel's own health.</dd>
+          <dt>Still open</dt><dd>The grain of the reject detail SEI returns on the poll. Record level gives an exception workbench; file level gives a status table. The screens are drawn at record level.</dd>
+          <dt>Also needs</dt><dd>An outbound expectation model. Nothing today records which loaders were <em>due</em>, so this view can show absence but never lateness.</dd>
+        </dl>
+      </div>
+
+      <button class="comp" aria-expanded="false"><span class="comp-dot d-ok"></span><span class="comp-n">ExceptionList</span></button>
+      <div class="detail" hidden>
+        <h5>ExceptionList</h5>
+        <div class="dmeta"><span class="pill p-ok">specified</span><span>read-only</span></div>
+        <p>One list over three sources with different vocabularies: row-level business DQ from <code>DQ_VALIDATION_FAILURE</code>, structural test failures from dbt, and quarantine and transport errors from the Hub. Presented as one classification; each row keeps its source's own value verbatim.</p>
+        <dl>
+          <dt>Cannot</dt><dd>Resolve, waive, retry or acknowledge. The Hub owns the lifecycle; this shows its state.</dd>
+          <dt>Sorts by</dt><dd>Days to expiry first — an OPEN reprocess-eligible row approaching the INT retention edge is a transaction about to be lost permanently.</dd>
+          <dt>Naming</dt><dd>The SEI diagram labels this capability "Exception Management". It is exception <em>visibility</em>. Worth aligning the words before someone plans against the label.</dd>
+        </dl>
+      </div>
+
+      <button class="comp" aria-expanded="false"><span class="comp-dot d-stop"></span><span class="comp-n">LiveFlowBoard</span></button>
+      <div class="detail" hidden>
+        <h5>LiveFlowBoard</h5>
+        <div class="dmeta"><span class="pill p-stop">blocked</span><span>the intraday clock</span></div>
+        <p>Micro-batch by partition as a grid, with consumer lag per partition, the last batch received per topic, and current backlog. Localises a stall to a partition immediately — the difference between "the event channel is behind" and "partition 2 stopped at 04:12".</p>
+        <dl>
+          <dt>Reads</dt><dd><code>GET /int360/event/live</code></dd>
+          <dt>Refreshes</dt><dd>Continuously. This view has no notion of completeness — only flowing or falling behind.</dd>
+          <dt>Blocked on</dt><dd>Partition count per domain topic. Without a denominator, "all partitions reported" cannot be distinguished from "the ones I happened to see".</dd>
+        </dl>
+      </div>
+
+      <button class="comp" aria-expanded="false"><span class="comp-dot d-ok"></span><span class="comp-n">StageFunnel</span></button>
+      <div class="detail" hidden>
+        <h5>StageFunnel</h5>
+        <div class="dmeta"><span class="pill p-ok">specified</span><span>the correctness view</span></div>
+        <p>Six numbers for one business date that must tie: events received → distinct keys → rows pulled → Stage 1 rows → INT rows → Gold rows, with the explained deduction at each step — duplicates collapsed, not-found keys, source-DQ filtered, missing-dimension held.</p>
+        <dl>
+          <dt>Reads</dt><dd><code>GET /int360/funnel/{date}</code></dd>
+          <dt>Why it matters</dt><dd>Anything unexplained is the number worth looking at. It joins the four event-side boundaries onto the medallion's existing four so the chain reads as one funnel rather than two disconnected halves.</dd>
+        </dl>
+      </div>
+
+      <button class="comp" aria-expanded="false"><span class="comp-dot d-ok"></span><span class="comp-n">BusinessDateTimeline</span></button>
+      <div class="detail" hidden>
+        <h5>BusinessDateTimeline</h5>
+        <div class="dmeta"><span class="pill p-ok">specified</span><span>the drill path</span></div>
+        <p>One date expanded: intraday cycles, the micro-batches inside each, the gate transition, then the transformation tasks. The level of detail is "the transformation failed at <code>build_dim</code>, attempt 2, at 04:12" — then a deep link into the Airflow UI for the log.</p>
+        <dl>
+          <dt>Boundary</dt><dd>Deliberately does not reproduce Airflow's task view. That UI exists, it is good, and it is one tab away.</dd>
+        </dl>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+
+  <!-- PLANE: ROUTERS -->
+  <div class="plane">
+    <div class="plane-hd">
+      <div class="plane-t"><span class="glyph"></span>Read API · Routers</div>
+      <div class="plane-meta">6 components · mounted by name</div>
+    </div>
+    <div class="comps">
+      <button class="comp" aria-expanded="false"><span class="comp-dot d-ok"></span><span class="comp-n">estate_router</span></button>
+      <div class="detail" hidden>
+        <h5>estate_router</h5>
+        <div class="dmeta"><span class="pill p-ok">specified</span></div>
