+  <div class="eyebrow">Detail</div>
+  <h1>Data model</h1>
+  <p class="lede">Integration360 owns five tables. Everything else it shows belongs to the system that produced it, and is read where it lives.</p>
+
+  <div class="note">
+    <span class="note-h">The test</span>
+    <p>If Integration360's schema grows beyond derived state, something has leaked across the boundary. No table here would ever need to be read back by the pipeline, the Hub or SEI.</p>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2>Owned</h2>
+  <div class="tscroll"><table>
+    <thead><tr><th style="width:24%">Table</th><th style="width:30%">Key</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead>
+    <tbody>
+      <tr><td><code>int360_verdict_snapshot</code></td><td><code>(channel, business_date, verdict_type)</code></td><td>Derived value, threshold in force, inputs, computed timestamp. The threshold is the column that stops this system disagreeing with Splunk.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><code>int360_rollup_daily</code></td><td><code>(channel, business_date)</code></td><td>Counts, durations, exception totals, reconciliation differences. Also the precomputed cross-source correlation, since no SQL join spans the sources.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><code>int360_alert_log</code></td><td><code>(finding_key, fired_ts)</code></td><td>What was dispatched, to whom, acknowledged or not.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><code>int360_saved_view</code></td><td><code>(user_id, view_name)</code></td><td>Filters and layout per user.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><code>int360_annotation</code> <span class="pill p-warn">optional</span></td><td><code>(observation_ref, created_ts)</code></td><td>Operator notes attached to an observation, never to a pipeline record.</td></tr>
+    </tbody>
+  </table></div>
+
+  <h2>Read, not owned</h2>
+  <div class="tscroll"><table>
+    <thead><tr><th style="width:20%">Source</th><th style="width:34%">Objects</th><th>Answers</th></tr></thead>
+    <tbody>
+      <tr><td><strong>Oracle control</strong><br><span style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--ink-3)">Airflow + dbt</span></td><td><code>FILE_SCHEMA_CONFIG</code>, <code>FILE_REGISTRY</code>, <code>DATE_CONTROL</code>, <code>SDC_VIEW_CONFIG</code>, <code>SDC_EVENT_CONFIG</code>, <code>SDC_STAGED_ID</code>, <code>SDC_MICROBATCH_REGISTRY</code>, <code>SDC_INTRADAY_CYCLE</code>, <code>DQ_VALIDATION_FAILURE</code>, <code>RECON_RESULT</code></td><td>Completeness and correctness for both inbound channels; the exception backlog; every reconciliation boundary the pipeline computes.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Airflow</strong><br><span style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--ink-3)">Postgres replica</span></td><td><code>dag_run</code>, <code>task_instance</code>, <code>task_fail</code></td><td>Which task failed, on which attempt, at what time. Not log text — that lives wherever remote logging points.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Integration Hub</strong></td><td>Quarantine, transport errors, outbound submissions <span class="pill p-stop">location unknown</span></td><td>What the Hub is holding, what it retried, what it sent.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Notification inbox</strong><br><span style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--ink-3)">shared</span></td><td>Pushed SEI notifications with a per-consumer progress marker</td><td>Loader status transitions and error summaries. Reject detail is not here — it arrives on the poll leg and is stored against the submission.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>dbt</strong></td><td>Run results persisted by <code>on-run-end</code></td><td>Model-level status and test outcomes — how a reconciliation break gets explained.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Landing zone</strong></td><td>Standby file header and trailer only <span class="pill p-stop">mount unresolved</span></td><td>The declared row count, for the cross-channel check.</td></tr>
+    </tbody>
+  </table></div>
+
+  <h2>Two kinds of data quality, kept apart</h2>
+  <div class="grid2">
+    <div class="card"><h4>Row-level business DQ</h4><p><code>DQ_VALIDATION_FAILURE</code>. This account has an unmapped status code. Grain: one failing record.</p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Model-level structural DQ</h4><p>dbt test results. Uniqueness, not-null, referential integrity, one open row per natural key. Grain: one assertion over a model.</p></div>
+  </div>
+  <p>An operator wants both on one screen. Merging them into one list makes both harder to read — so they are presented together and classified separately, the same discipline as keeping native status beside normalised status.</p>
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============ CHANNELS ============ -->
+<section class="view" id="v-channels" hidden>
+<div class="wrap">
+  <div class="eyebrow">Detail</div>
+  <h1>Four channels</h1>
+  <p class="lede">The channels are at very different maturities, and the design reflects that rather than pretending they are symmetrical.</p>
+
+  <div class="tscroll"><table>
+    <thead><tr><th style="width:14%">Channel</th><th style="width:11%">Direction</th><th style="width:16%">Status</th><th>What Integration360 can answer</th></tr></thead>
+    <tbody>
+      <tr>
+        <td><strong>Event</strong></td><td>SEI → BBH</td><td><span class="pill p-acc">primary ingestion</span></td>
+        <td>Micro-batch completeness, consumer lag per partition, sequence gaps, marker coverage, pull-complete gating. <em>Cannot</em> say what changed — the envelope carries no data and the pull re-reads current state.</td>
+      </tr>
+      <tr>
+        <td><strong>File</strong></td><td>SEI → BBH</td><td><span class="pill p-ok">daily standby</span></td>
+        <td>Arrival and readiness of the fallback, plus the trailer count for the cross-channel check. Answers "is the parachute packed", not "did the data arrive".</td>
+      </tr>
+      <tr>
+        <td><strong>Loader</strong></td><td>BBH → SEI</td><td><span class="pill p-warn">push + poll</span></td>
+        <td>Submission lifecycle, accepted and rejected counts, reject detail, the template version in force, and whether each status arrived by push or by poll. Still depends on the Hub recording the send.</td>
+      </tr>
+      <tr>
+        <td><strong>API</strong></td><td>both</td><td><span class="pill p-ext">not Day 1</span></td>
+        <td>Nothing yet. Day 1 is file and event based; no API traffic exists to observe. Its first production member is the loader callback endpoint itself.</td>
+      </tr>
+    </tbody>
+  </table></div>
+
+  <h2>The taxonomy is two dimensions, not one</h2>
+  <p>FILE, API and EVENT are <strong>transports</strong>. LOADER is a <strong>business flow</strong> — and it travels over the API transport. Treating channel as a single top-level partition forces every loader record to misrepresent one of them.</p>
+  <p>There is a third dimension too. Some events carry data; markers, file-ready signals and the 51 technical events carry <strong>control</strong>. That axis decides whether an event is something to ingest or something to display, so it belongs in the model rather than in a comment.</p>
+
+  <div class="grid2">
+    <div class="card"><h4>Transport</h4><p>FILE · API · EVENT — how the bytes moved.</p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Flow</h4><p>Inbound feed · loader submission · status callback — what the message is for.</p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Plane</h4><p>Data or control — whether it is ingested or merely observed.</p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Cost of getting it wrong</h4><p>One column now. A migration across every observation record later.</p></div>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2>Completeness works differently per channel</h2>
+  <div class="tscroll"><table>
+    <thead><tr><th style="width:16%">Channel</th><th style="width:34%">Completeness signal</th><th>Strength</th></tr></thead>
+    <tbody>
+      <tr><td>Event</td><td>Every in-scope domain markered, every micro-batch <code>LOADED</code>, every batch reconciled, no unexplained not-found keys</td><td>Strong — sequence numbers are monotonic per partition, so a gap is a provably lost event</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>File</td><td>Expected interfaces minus those in the released state</td><td>Strong, but the readiness state is an archive move outside the load transaction — a storage blip looks like missing data</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Loader</td><td>Submitted set versus terminal set, from notification or poll, whichever arrives</td><td>Moderate — the poll closes the silence gap a pure push leaves, but it still requires the Hub to record the send</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>API</td><td>None</td><td>No traffic</td></tr>
+    </tbody>
+  </table></div>
+
+  <div class="note warn">
+    <span class="note-h">The marker is not the gate</span>
+    <p>A marker means SEI finished publishing. It says nothing about BBH finishing consuming. The gate opens on <strong>pull complete</strong> — markers received, every micro-batch loaded, every batch reconciled. Gating on the marker alone would look like a control and be a comfort.</p>
+  </div>
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============ OBSERVATION MODEL ============ -->
