+  </table></div>
+
+  <h2>The funnel</h2>
+  <p>Six numbers for one business date that must tie, each with its explained deduction. Anything unexplained is the number worth looking at.</p>
+  <div class="tscroll"><table>
+    <thead><tr><th style="width:26%">Stage</th><th style="width:34%">Explained deduction</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
+    <tbody>
+      <tr><td>Events received</td><td>—</td><td><code>SDC_STAGED_ID</code></td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Distinct keys</td><td>duplicates collapsed</td><td>micro-batch registry</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Rows pulled</td><td>not-found keys</td><td>micro-batch registry</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Stage 1 rows</td><td>— must equal rows pulled</td><td>micro-batch registry</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>INT rows</td><td>source-DQ filtered</td><td><code>RECON_RESULT</code></td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Gold rows</td><td>missing-dimension held</td><td><code>RECON_RESULT</code> + DQ store</td></tr>
+    </tbody>
+  </table></div>
+  <p>This puts the four event-side boundaries in front of the medallion’s existing four, so the chain reads as one funnel rather than two disconnected halves.</p>
+
+  <h2>Timeliness without an expectation table</h2>
+  <p>For the event channel, lateness is derivable from the stream’s own rhythm. Micro-batch IDs are sequential and arrive at a characteristic interval, so a rolling baseline per topic turns <em>"MB 1049 has not arrived"</em> into <em>"MB 1049 is 40 minutes overdue against a 6-minute norm"</em>. That is <code>cadence_baseline</code>, and it needs nothing from SEI.</p>
+  <p><strong>EOD is the exception.</strong> There is one marker a day and no rhythm to baseline against, so its expected arrival time still has to be stated — the one place the expectation model is unavoidable on this channel.</p>
+
+  <div class="note warn">
+    <span class="note-h">The failure mode this architecture creates</span>
+    <p>Intraday micro-batches load into Stage 1 all day while <code>DATE_CONTROL</code> sits at <code>PENDING</code>, and the transformation runs once at EOD over whatever accumulated. So a micro-batch that failed at 11am and went unnoticed means the EOD transformation runs on <strong>incomplete Stage 1</strong> — and the reconciliation boundaries will not necessarily catch it, because STG→INT ties perfectly against a Stage 1 that is itself short.</p>
+    <p>Two consequences. Intraday needs its own alerting rather than only feeding the EOD gate. And the gate must require every micro-batch for the date to be <code>LOADED</code>, not merely that the EOD marker arrived.</p>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2>What this cannot show</h2>
+  <p>The event carries no data and the pull re-reads current state, so multiple intraday changes to one key collapse into one observation. Integration360 can say a key was touched in micro-batch 1047 and again in 1051; it cannot say what changed, and cannot reconstruct the intermediate state.</p>
+  <p>Lineage runs <strong>Gold row → micro-batch → key</strong>, never Gold row → the specific change that caused it. Worth stating plainly, because "near-real-time event streaming" sets an expectation of change-level traceability this pattern deliberately does not provide.</p>
+
+  <h2>The four screens</h2>
+  <div class="grid2">
+    <div class="card"><h4>Live flow — the intraday clock</h4><p>Micro-batch by partition as a grid, lag per partition, last batch per topic, current backlog. Localises a stall to a partition. <code>LiveFlowBoard</code></p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Business date — the EOD clock</h4><p>EOD marker, micro-batch tally, transformation task chain, reconciliation, DQ, <code>DATE_CONTROL</code> state. <code>BusinessDateTimeline</code></p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Funnel</h4><p>The six numbers and their explained deductions for one date. <code>StageFunnel</code></p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Exceptions</h4><p>DQ holds, not-found keys, failed micro-batches — days-to-expiry first. <code>ExceptionList</code></p></div>
+  </div>
+
+  <div class="note">
+    <span class="note-h">One model change this requires</span>
+    <p>The micro-batch now has two levels, because MB Start and End are written to <em>every partition of every domain topic</em>. A partition-level record holds the boxing; a parent record holds the logical batch and is what the gate aggregates against. The existing registry keyed on <code>(topic, partition, mb_id)</code> is the partition level — the parent is missing and needs adding.</p>
+  </div>
+</div>
+</section>
