+      <tr><td><span class="pill" style="background:#6d3ac01f;color:#6d3ac0">GET</span></td><td><code>/dq/rules</code></td><td>The rule registry — gate, scope, blocking per rule, cost class.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><span class="pill" style="background:#6d3ac01f;color:#6d3ac0">GET</span></td><td><code>/dq/results</code></td><td>Evidence a rule ran, including when it found nothing. <code>NOT_RUN</code> is a real verdict.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><span class="pill" style="background:#6d3ac01f;color:#6d3ac0">GET</span></td><td><code>/dq/failures</code></td><td>Failing records with <code>days_to_expiry</code>.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td colspan="3" style="background:var(--sunk);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.3px;color:#159943">RECONCILIATION</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><span class="pill" style="background:#1599431f;color:#159943">GET</span></td><td><code>/reconciliation/{date}</code></td><td>All twelve boundaries with the tolerance in force alongside each verdict.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><span class="pill" style="background:#1599431f;color:#159943">GET</span></td><td><code>/reconciliation/{date}/{boundary}</code></td><td>One boundary, both legs, every deduction named.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td colspan="3" style="background:var(--sunk);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.3px;color:#10193b">INBOUND · THE ONLY WRITE</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><span class="pill" style="background:#10193b;color:#fff">POST</span></td><td><code>/sei/notifications</code></td><td>SEI pushes a status transition or an error summary. 202 on durable write, never on successful processing.</td></tr>
+    </tbody>
+  </table></div>
+
+  <h2>Rules that hold across every path</h2>
+  <div class="grid3">
+    <div class="card"><h4>Read-only, one exception</h4><p>Every path is a GET except <code>POST /sei/notifications</code>, and that write lands in an inbox the Hub owns. Integration360 is a second consumer and is never on the remediation path.</p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Redaction on read</h4><p>Business keys are masked on the way out, not at rest, so a wrong mask can be corrected without having destroyed the original. Unmasking needs <code>int360.pii.read</code>.</p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Cursor paging only</h4><p>No offsets. The underlying tables grow while a caller pages, and an offset silently skips rows when they do.</p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>UNKNOWN is never GOOD</h4><p>When an input is unavailable the verdict is <code>UNKNOWN</code>. A missing Airflow replica returns 503 for that path rather than a green estate board.</p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Thresholds travel with verdicts</h4><p>Every verdict carries <code>threshold_in_force</code> or <code>tolerance_in_force</code>, so it stays reproducible after the threshold moves and cannot silently diverge from Splunk.</p></div>
+    <div class="card"><h4>Native codes survive</h4><p>SEI's code is kept verbatim beside the normalised one. An unmapped value raises rather than being mapped to its nearest neighbour.</p></div>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2>The one write, in detail</h2>
+  <ol class="steps">
+    <li><strong>Acknowledge on durable write.</strong> Authenticate, validate the envelope shape, store the raw body plus receipt metadata, return 202. Parsing, correlation and status derivation all happen downstream. A 500 because a lookup failed would tie SEI's retry behaviour to BBH's internal bugs.</li>
+    <li><strong>Generic by design.</strong> <code>message_type</code> is an open string and <code>payload</code> is unconstrained, so a new status or counter needs no contract change. An unrecognised type is stored and alerted, never rejected.</li>
+    <li><strong>Deduplication keys on <code>message_id</code></strong>, which must be stable across SEI's own retries. An HTTP callback has no equivalent of the event side's <code>(topic, partition, offset)</code>. Without it, a retried "50 records rejected" becomes 100 in the exception count.</li>
+    <li><strong>Thin.</strong> A status transition and an error <em>summary</em> travel here. Reject detail does not — it is fetched on the poll leg, paginated and stored.</li>
+    <li><strong>409 is idempotent.</strong> A duplicate <code>message_id</code> returns the original receipt and writes nothing twice.</li>
+  </ol>
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============ RUNTIME ============ -->
+<section class="view" id="v-runtime" hidden>
+<div class="wrap">
+  <div class="eyebrow">Detail</div>
+  <h1>Runtime flows</h1>
+  <p class="lede">Three sequences carry most of the design's behaviour. Everything else is a read.</p>
+
+  <h2>1 · The outbound loader loop — submit, notify, poll</h2>
+  <p>Three legs, all owned by the Integration Hub. <strong>Push notifies; poll fetches detail and backstops a notification that never arrives.</strong> The important property is that Integration360 is nowhere in the acknowledgement path and never calls SEI. It can be down, mid-deploy or failing over and neither SEI nor the Hub notices.</p>
+
+  <h3 style="font-size:14px;margin:18px 0 6px">Leg 1 — BBH submits</h3>
+  <ol class="steps">
+    <li>The Hub generates the loader file to SEI's template and submits it through the Apigee proxy.</li>
+    <li>The submission identifier returned here is the correlation key every later leg hangs off. <em>The Hub must record the submission at send time</em> — without that row, a submission that never reached SEI is indistinguishable from one that succeeded.</li>
+  </ol>
+
+  <h3 style="font-size:14px;margin:18px 0 6px">Leg 2 — SEI notifies (push)</h3>
+  <ol class="steps">
+    <li><strong>SEI Workflows</strong> posts a status transition and an error summary to the BBH Apigee proxy (node 12). Thin and event-shaped — reject detail does not travel here.</li>
+    <li>The proxy authenticates, enforces rate limits, and passes it on. It rejects only for authentication or a malformed envelope — never for a business reason.</li>
+    <li>The message is written to the <strong>notification inbox</strong>, encrypted at rest, and acknowledged with 202. <em>Acknowledge on durable write, not on successful processing</em> — parsing, correlation and status derivation all happen downstream of that write. A 500 because a correlation lookup failed ties SEI's retry behaviour to BBH's internal bugs.</li>
+    <li>Dedupe keys on a SEI-supplied message identifier stable across retries. Anything unparseable becomes a dead-letter row with an alert; nothing is dropped.</li>
+    <li>The <strong>Integration Hub</strong> consumes the inbox to remediate, tracking its own progress marker. <strong>Integration360</strong> consumes it independently to display, tracking a separate marker. Neither consumer blocks the other.</li>
+  </ol>
+
+  <h3 style="font-size:14px;margin:18px 0 6px">Leg 3 — BBH polls (backstop and detail)</h3>
+  <ol class="steps">
+    <li>The Hub's monitoring worker selects <strong>non-terminal submissions only</strong>. A submission that reached a terminal state by either source drops out of the poll set, so call volume tracks work in flight rather than history.</li>
+    <li>Cadence is tiered: tight for the first interval after submission, backing off after. A loader SEI processes in four minutes is not polled every five minutes until midnight.</li>
+    <li>On reaching a terminal-with-errors state, reject detail is fetched <strong>once</strong>, paginated, and stored. Fetching errors on every poll would pull the same rejected records repeatedly.</li>
+    <li>A submission still non-terminal past its max age becomes <code>STATUS_UNRESOLVED</code> — the outbound equivalent of a business date that never reaches <code>COMPLETE</code>.</li>
+  </ol>
+
+  <div class="note">
+    <span class="note-h">When the two sources disagree, the poll wins</span>
+    <p>A poll is a read of SEI's current state; a notification is a point-in-time event that can arrive late, out of order, or twice. History keeps every row from both with its <code>SOURCE</code>; the current status is derived by <code>status_precedence</code>, and terminal never regresses.</p>
+    <p>The pairing also closes the hole a pure push model leaves. Under push alone, a submission whose notification never arrives sits at <code>SUBMITTED</code> and the failure is invisible unless something holds an expectation of it. The poller makes that a detected condition — and the gap between the two sources becomes the running measure of the push channel's own health.</p>
+  </div>
+
+  <h2>2 · Nightly verdict computation</h2>
+  <p>Verdicts are computed once, stored with their inputs, and read back — rather than recomputed on every page load and never kept.</p>
+  <ol class="steps">
+    <li>The Observation Engine reads the closed business date from <code>DATE_CONTROL</code>.</li>
+    <li><code>expectation_evaluator</code> establishes what was due for that date, per channel. <span class="pill p-stop">blocked — no expectation model exists</span></li>
+    <li><code>verdict_computer</code> derives SLA state against the cutoff, and reconciliation verdicts from the boundary counts, applying the tolerance <strong>read from shared configuration</strong>.</li>
+    <li>Each verdict is written to <code>int360_verdict_snapshot</code> <strong>with the threshold that was in force</strong>, so it can be reproduced and defended later.</li>
+    <li><code>rollup_builder</code> writes the daily aggregate, including cross-source correlations that no SQL join could produce.</li>
+    <li><code>alert_evaluator</code> compares findings against <code>int360_alert_log</code> and dispatches only what is new.</li>
+  </ol>
+
+  <h2>3 · Cross-channel reconciliation</h2>
+  <p>The check that only exists because both paths deliver the same data every day. It costs two lines of a file.</p>
+  <ol class="steps">
+    <li><code>landing_trailer_reader</code> parses the header and trailer of the standby file for an interface and date. The file is never loaded.</li>
+    <li>The declared trailer count is compared against the events received for the matching domain and date, and against the count declared by the marker.</li>
+    <li>Three numbers that must agree. Any difference is written as a reconciliation row with all three legs visible.</li>
+    <li>A difference means opposite things depending on which side is authoritative — an event defect or a file defect. <span class="pill p-stop">blocked — authority unresolved</span></li>
+  </ol>
+
+  <div class="note">
+    <span class="note-h">Why this is the flagship</span>
+    <p>A missing file is obvious. A stream that quietly dropped three percent of ACCOUNT events is not, and no other control in the estate would catch it. Under the standby arrangement the second dataset is already arriving; this check is nearly free, and it is the only independent proof that the primary ingestion path is complete.</p>
+    <p>It also has a shelf life. If standby file generation sunsets once events stabilise, this capability goes with it — so it should not be the spine of the product unless standby is permanent.</p>
+  </div>
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============ OPEN ============ -->
+<section class="view" id="v-open" hidden>
+<div class="wrap">
+  <div class="eyebrow">Detail</div>
+  <h1>Assumptions and blockers</h1>
+  <p class="lede">Four components are blocked, down from seven. The Hub’s Foundation framework closed three of them. Three of the four that remain resolve with a written answer rather than engineering — which makes this dependency management, not a technical problem.</p>
+
+  <h2>Blocks the design</h2>
+  <div class="tscroll"><table>
+    <thead><tr><th style="width:26%">Open item</th><th style="width:17%">Blocks</th><th>What changes on the answer</th></tr></thead>
+    <tbody>
+      <tr><td><strong>SEI's status API contract</strong> — the poll leg</td><td><code>loader_status_reader</code>, and the Hub's monitoring worker</td><td>Endpoint and auth through Apigee, whether reject detail is the same endpoint or separate, pagination shape, rate limits, and whether a mid-processing poll returns partial errors. Settles the poller's budget and its cadence.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Reject detail retention at SEI</strong> — how long after terminal it stays retrievable</td><td><code>loader_status_reader</code></td><td>If detail is purged after a window, the fetch has a deadline and a missed fetch is permanent loss. That turns the persisted copy from a convenience into the system of record for outbound exceptions.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>The expectation model</strong> — what was due today, on any channel</td><td><code>expectation_evaluator</code></td><td>Without it nothing can be late, only absent. One table covering inbound feeds, loaders and markers closes all three at once, because the marker gives events the same shape as files.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Threshold configuration</strong> — where recon tolerance and the SLA warning percentage live</td><td><code>verdict_computer</code></td><td>Verdicts can be shown but not coloured, and this system may contradict Splunk on the same date with no way to adjudicate.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Who uses this</strong></td><td>Every UI component</td><td>No user research exists. Competent default screens are achievable; the right ones are not. This is internal, costs one conversation, and nobody has scheduled it.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>Airflow replica and API auth</strong></td><td><code>airflow_reader</code></td><td>Without it, failure attribution stops at "the business date is stuck" and never reaches "<code>build_dim</code> failed on attempt 2".</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><strong>The staging store specification</strong></td><td><code>event_router</code></td><td>Entirely BBH-owned and nobody is writing it. If it ships without <code>(topic, partition, offset, enqueued_ts, sequence_number)</code>, lag and gap detection are not computable at all.</td></tr>
+    </tbody>
+  </table></div>
+
+  <h2>Waiting on SEI</h2>
+  <div class="tscroll"><table>
+    <thead><tr><th style="width:34%">Question</th><th>Consequence for this design</th></tr></thead>
+    <tbody>
+      <tr><td>Does the marker carry a declared record count?</td><td>With a count, the cross-channel check has three legs and the gate is safe against cross-partition ordering. Without, it degrades to two and relies on an ordering guarantee Event Hub does not give.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Marker grain — per view, per domain, or per date?</td><td>Sets how many signals the gate waits on. Twelve source tables and twelve marker events suggests one per table, but that is inference from matching counts, not something stated.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Names and meanings of the 51 Technical events</td><td>The largest category in the catalogue. If they report SEI-side processing state they are monitoring signal rather than data, and the only window past the boundary.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Which side is authoritative — file or event?</td><td>Decides whether a cross-channel difference is an event defect or a file defect. Opposite operational responses.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Event Hub retention and partition count</td><td>Retention is the recovery window and therefore the RTO. Partition count is the throughput ceiling and the shape of the lag display.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>A message identifier stable across redelivery</td><td>At-least-once on the event side is handled by <code>(topic, partition, offset)</code>; an HTTP notification has no equivalent. Either SEI supplies a <code>message_id</code> that survives its own retry, or it guarantees byte-identical redelivery so the body can be hashed. Without one, a retried "50 records rejected" becomes 100 rejected records in the exception count.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Endpoint scope — loader callbacks only, or SDC platform events too?</td><td>Decides whether the generic envelope BBH defines carries one message family or two. Cheaper to answer now than to version the contract later.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Callback retry behaviour on 5xx</td><td>Sets BBH's availability obligation. With the durable inbox in place this stops being existential, which is most of why the inbox is worth building.</td></tr>
+    </tbody>
+  </table></div>
+
+  <h2>Assumptions this design rests on</h2>
+  <div class="tscroll"><table>
+    <thead><tr><th style="width:34%">Assumption</th><th>If wrong</th></tr></thead>
+    <tbody>
+      <tr><td><code>RECON_RESULT</code> gains a <code>MODEL_NAME</code> column</td><td>Account, client and transaction results collide on one key. No per-model reconciliation drill-down is possible.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>dbt persists run results to Oracle</td><td>Model-level outcomes and test results become a filesystem dependency, or are lost. A reconciliation break gets much harder to explain.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><code>FILE_REGISTRY</code> gains a state meaning "available and deliberately unused"</td><td>Standby files look either permanently missing or falsely satisfy a completeness gate that should not be running.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td><code>DATE_CONTROL</code> records which path satisfied the date</td><td>The provenance of a date's data is unrecoverable, and a discrepancy three weeks later cannot be explained.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>Integration360 gets a landing-zone read mount</td><td>The cross-channel check needs an upstream component to publish the trailer count instead — a different deployment and a different owner.</td></tr>
+      <tr><td>The Hub records a submission at send time</td><td>Half the sent-versus-accepted reconciliation does not exist, and a submission that never reached SEI is indistinguishable from one that succeeded.</td></tr>
+    </tbody>
+  </table></div>
+
+  <hr class="rule">
+  <p style="color:var(--ink-3);font-size:12.5px">Every schema change named above is free while nothing is deployed, and becomes a change request afterwards. That is the argument for settling them now rather than in build.</p>
+</div>
+</section>
+
+</main>
+</div>
+
+<script>
+(function(){
+  var views = {}, btns = [].slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.navbtn'));
+  [].forEach.call(document.querySelectorAll('.view'), function(v){ views[v.id.slice(2)] = v; });
+
+  function show(name, push){
+    if(!views[name]) name = 'overview';
+    for(var k in views) views[k].hidden = (k !== name);
+    btns.forEach(function(b){ b.setAttribute('aria-current', b.dataset.view === name ? 'true' : 'false'); });
+    if(push !== false){ try{ history.replaceState(null,'','#'+name); }catch(e){} }
+    window.scrollTo(0,0);
+  }
+
+  btns.forEach(function(b){ b.addEventListener('click', function(){ show(b.dataset.view); }); });
+
+  [].forEach.call(document.querySelectorAll('[data-jump]'), function(a){
+    a.addEventListener('click', function(e){ e.preventDefault(); show(a.dataset.jump); });
+  });
+
+  // component expand/collapse
+  [].forEach.call(document.querySelectorAll('.comp'), function(btn){
+    var panel = btn.nextElementSibling;
+    if(!panel || !panel.classList.contains('detail')) return;
+    btn.setAttribute('aria-expanded','false');
+    btn.addEventListener('click', function(){
+      var open = btn.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true';
+      // close siblings within the same plane
+      var plane = btn.closest('.plane');
+      [].forEach.call(plane.querySelectorAll('.comp'), function(o){
+        o.setAttribute('aria-expanded','false');
+        if(o.nextElementSibling && o.nextElementSibling.classList.contains('detail')) o.nextElementSibling.hidden = true;
+      });
+      if(!open){ btn.setAttribute('aria-expanded','true'); panel.hidden = false; }
+    });
+  });
+
+  // theme toggle
+  var tb = document.getElementById('themebtn');
+  function current(){
+    var a = document.documentElement.getAttribute('data-theme');
+    if(a) return a;
+    return window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches ? 'dark' : 'light';
+  }
+  tb.addEventListener('click', function(){
+    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', current() === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark');
+  });
+
+  var h = (location.hash || '').replace('#','');
+  show(h || 'overview', false);
+})();
+</script>
